class Rectangle {
    private double length, breadth;
    void set(double l, double b) { length = l; breadth = b; }
    double area() { return length * breadth; }
    double perimeter() { return 2 * (length + breadth); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rectangle r = new Rectangle();
        r.set(4, 3);
        System.out.println(r.area() + " " + r.perimeter());
    }
}
Output: 12.0 14.0
Encapsulation: the fields are private and can be changed only through set(), so data and the methods that use it are bundled and protected.
Abstraction: a user simply calls area() or perimeter() without knowing the formulas inside.

Wrapper classes (Integer, Double, Character, Boolean, etc.) are classes in java.lang that wrap a primitive value in an object, so primitives can be used where objects are required (e.g. collections) and so that utility methods such as Integer.parseInt() are available.
Autoboxing: automatic conversion of a primitive to its wrapper — Integer obj = 25;
Unboxing: automatic conversion of a wrapper back to the primitive — int n = obj + 5; // n = 30

