static String titleCase(String s) {
    String[] w = s.trim().split(" ");
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    for (int i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
        if (w[i].length() == 0) continue;
        sb.append(Character.toUpperCase(w[i].charAt(0)));
        sb.append(w[i].substring(1).toLowerCase());
        if (i < w.length - 1) sb.append(' ');
    }
    return sb.toString();
}
static boolean isPalindrome(String w) {
    w = w.toLowerCase();
    int i = 0, j = w.length() - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        if (w.charAt(i) != w.charAt(j)) return false;
        i++; j--;
    }
    return true;
}
titleCase("tHE ISC exam") returns "The Isc Exam"; isPalindrome("Madam") returns true.

static int countDigit(int n, int d) {
    if (n == 0) return 0;
    int add = (n % 10 == d) ? 1 : 0;
    return add + countDigit(n / 10, d);
}
Digits of 737173 are 7, 3, 7, 1, 7, 3, so countDigit(737173, 7) = 3.

Comparison: (1) Recursion uses method calls; iteration uses loops. (2) Recursion needs a base case; iteration needs a loop condition. (3) Recursion uses stack memory for every pending call and can overflow; iteration uses constant extra memory. (4) Recursion is often shorter and closer to the mathematical definition; iteration is usually faster.
static int fibR(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fibR(n - 1) + fibR(n - 2);
}
static int fibI(int n) {
    int a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { int c = a + b; a = b; b = c; }
    return a;
}
The recursive version recomputes the same subproblems repeatedly (fibR(n − 2) is computed by both branches), so the number of calls grows exponentially (fibR(30) makes over 2.6 million calls), whereas the loop needs only n iterations.
fib(10) = 55.

