try encloses code that may throw an exception. catch(Type e) handles a matching exception; several catch blocks can follow, from specific to general. finally contains clean-up code that runs whether or not an exception occurred.
Example:
try {
    int r = 10 / 0;
    System.out.println(r);
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
} finally {
    System.out.println("Done");
}
Output:
Cannot divide by zero
Done

import java.util.*;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int count = 0, sum = 0;
        while (true) {
            String tok = sc.next();
            int n;
            try {
                n = Integer.parseInt(tok);
            } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
                System.out.println("Skipping " + tok);
                continue;
            }
            if (n == 0) break;
            sum += n;
            count++;
        }
        System.out.printf("Count=%d Average=%.2f%n", count, (double) sum / count);
    }
}
